    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d248">

                <pb facs="0447" n="361" sameAs="frus:frus1945Malta/pg361"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President to the
                        Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d248-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Excerpts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-10-04">4 October 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Number 76. Top Secret and Personal from the President to Ambassador Harriman.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Will you please deliver the following message to Marshal Stalin at once:</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>You will, by this time, have received from General Deane, the statement of our Combined Chiefs of Staff position
                    relative to the war against Japan<anchor n="3" xml:id="d248-f2"/> and I want to
                    reiterate to you how completely I accept the assurances which you have given us
                    on this point. Our three countries are waging a successful war against Germany
                    and we can surely join together with no less success in crushing a nation that I
                    am sure in my heart is as great an enemy of Russia as she is of ours.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Roosevelt</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d248-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d248-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to the United States Naval Attaché, Moscow, via Navy channels. For the full
                    text of the message, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg6"
                        >pp. 6</ref>–<ref target="#pg7">7</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d248-f2" n="3" xml:id="d248-f2-content">
                <p>The President evidently was referring to the Joint Chiefs of Staff position
                    alluded to <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
